1.0 Generally.

1.1    Background.  The City of Arvada has for many years regulated the business use of residential properties. This includes residential properties used as short-term rentals (STRs).  STRs are residential living spaces available to rent for short periods of time.  Shorter than a traditional lease, a short-term rental period ranges from 2-29 consecutive days.  STRs are known by various names, including vacation rentals.  They are usually made available via a vacation rental platform, also known as a marketplace facilitator, that facilitates or brokers STR reservations online and receives payment for providing this service.  STRs exclude hotels and longer-term rentals.

1.2    STR program.  Arvada’s STR Program consists of the implementation, administration, and enforcement of the STR ordinance found at section 3-1-5-3 of Arvada’s Land Development Code (LDC), related portions of the LDC and the Arvada City Code, these Rules, those portions of the City’s Fees Schedule devoted to STR fees, and related activities.  Prospective and current STR permittees are encouraged to be familiar with these and other sections of the City Code that apply to properties that are offered for rent as or being used as an STR.

1.3    STR program goals.  The goals of the STR Program are to protect the health, safety, and welfare of STR occupants, preserve the residential character of neighborhoods, and provide guidance for STR permittees and emergency contacts (ECs) about how to comply with the law and these Rules.

1.4    Complaints.  Complaints about an STR or a property thought to be used as an STR may be filed in accordance with section 11.0 below.

2.0    Authority and purpose. 

2.1    Authority.  These administrative Rules have been promulgated in accordance with City Code sections 2-141 – 145.

2.2    Purpose.  These Rules set forth the standards, procedures, and supplemental information necessary to administer the STR program and provide clarity about the STR program for STR permittees and ECs.

2.3    Amendment of.  These Rules may be updated, amended, expanded, or otherwise revised in accordance with City Code sections 2-141 – 145.

3.0    Definitions.  The words, terms, and phrases defined in LDC section 11-3-3-3 that are used in these Rules have the same meaning as set forth in that section.  The following words, terms, and phrases used in these Rules shall have the following meanings unless the context clearly requires otherwise:

Complaint means a statement alleging a violation of LDC section 3-1-5-3, of these Rules, or another section of the City Code related to the operation of a permitted STR or a property suspected of being offered for rent or used as an STR.

Contact information means the physical address, email address, and telephone number of an applicant or a natural person or an entity named in a permit application.  In the case of a person filing a complaint about an STR, it means the complainant’s name and their email address or telephone number.

Day means a calendar day.

Good standing means a determination by the director that an applicant or permittee is at the time of the determination not the subject of a pending “Order to Show Cause and Notice to Appear,” a summons and complaint or red tag notice related to any STR in the city,  has submitted all requested documentation to the director, and has paid all outstanding fees, charges, fines, taxes, and other amounts due to the city as a result of applying for or holding an STR permit.

4.0    Permit application.

4.1    Generally.  An applicant must submit an STR permit application to the director on a form supplied by the director and available on the city’s website.  The director may require that the application and all required associated documents be submitted electronically.  Should the director determine that an application is not complete in all respects or that the applicant is not eligible to apply for an STR permit under LDC section 3-1-5-3, the director will return the application to the applicant, except that the application fee is non-refundable.  If the applicant is eligible to apply for an STR permit, the applicant may re-file a complete application with the submission of a re-filing fee as noted in the applicable Fees Schedule.  The applicant must supply any information deemed reasonably necessary by the director to complete the application process.  The applicant will certify that the information on the application and in the accompanying documents is accurate and truthful under penalty of law.

4.2    Required documentation generally.  Each applicant must submit the following documentation with their completed application form:

4.2.1    The STR property’s parcel information and property address;

4.2.2    A diagram or floor plan depicting the property for which the STR permit is being sought.  It must show all floors of the dwelling to be permitted both finished and unfinished, use of rooms or areas, door locations, egress window locations and the size of each egress window, the location of fire extinguishers, and the location of smoke and carbon monoxide detectors;

4.2.3    A written property maintenance plan including the permittee’s plan for mowing the lawn and removing weeds, for snow and ice removal, and for rubbish and garbage removal including the name and contact information for the permittee’s trash hauling service;

4.2.4    The completed and signed EC form;

4.2.5    An acknowledgement that the applicant understands that each STR permit is contingent upon continuing compliance with all State laws, and local ordinances including the city’s property maintenance, fire, and other building codes;

4.2.6    A completed STR Property Inspection Form;

4.2.7    Proof of ownership of the property.  This may be by a copy of a recorded deed, by a notarized letter signed by the permittee that they hold an ownership interest in the permitted property as the term ownership interest is defined in LDC section 11-3-3-1, or proof that the applicant is a named beneficiary of a trust that owns the property;

4.2.8    A certification that there are no deed restrictions on the property prohibiting it from being used as an STR;

4.2.9    Information about any pending or previous summonses or Orders to Show Cause issued by the director, a peace officer, or a code enforcement official to the applicant or to the designated EC during the past year relating to:

4.2.9.1    The current or any previous STR permit or STR license issued by the city to the applicant; and

4.2.9.2    Any Arvada property that was offered for rent, rented, or used as an STR which was or is owned in whole or in part by the applicant or in which the applicant had a property interest;

4.2.10    A valid government-issued identification or passport;

4.2.11    A valid City of Arvada business license;

4.2.12    Proof of insurance as required under LDC section 3-1-5-3;

4.2.13 The permit application fee and other fees as applicable including the annual permit fee; and

4.2.14    Any other documentation deemed necessary as determined by the director. 

4.3    Renewal application.  A permittee must file a renewal application form and must update documentation already in the permittee’s file as required by the director.  In addition, the director may require additional information reasonably necessary to confirm that the permittee has been and is in compliance with all applicable laws and these Rules.

4.4    Personally identifiable information.  By applying for an STR permit or by signing an EC designation form, each permittee and EC must acknowledge and agree that information that might identify them is being gathered for regulatory purposes as part of the city’s STR Program.  By applying for an STR permit or by signing an EC designation form, each permittee and EC must acknowledge that their names and names and telephone numbers may be released to the public, including but not limited to occupants of the permitted STR.  Financial information related to taxation will not be made available to the public except as otherwise noted in Chapter 98 of the City Code.

5.0    Actions by the director: approval of permits.

5.1 Generally.  In determining whether to approve, deny, not renew, suspend, or revoke a permit, the director may review and consider information submitted by the applicant, from any city employee, any city data system or contractor, from the Arvada Police Department or any other criminal justice agency or system, from a marketplace facilitator, or from the EC, and may consult with any other person or resource reasonably necessary to provide information relevant to the director’s decision.

5.1.1    Conditions.  The director may, as a condition of approval, retention, or renewal of any permit, impose on the permittee any reasonable condition or conditions related to the permit or the operation of the STR that is in accord with the intent of LDC section 3-1-5-3 and these Rules.

5.1.2.  Permit decisions - time limits.  The director should make any permit decision required under LDC section 3-1-5-3 or these Rules within 21 days of submission by the applicant of all information required to complete the STR permit application.  This deadline may be extended if the director must arrange to have the property inspected.

5.2    Approval of initial permit application.  The director may approve a permit or approve a permit with conditions if the director has sufficient information to satisfy the director that the applicant has submitted all required documentation, has met the requirements set forth in LDC section 3-1-5-3 and other applicable provisions of the City Code and these Rules, and is otherwise in good standing.

5.3    Approval of renewal permit application.  The director may approve the renewal of an STR permit or approve it with conditions if the applicant has met the approval requirements noted in subsection 5.2 above and has met the following requirements:

5.3.1    Provided information about any changes to the previous application for the permit;

5.3.2    Provided the number of days the STR was occupied by guests during the previous 365 days;

5.3.3    Submitted information from the city’s finance director that all lodging, sales, and other taxes due to the city have been paid as of the date the renewal application is filed with the city;

5.3.4    Submitted financial records necessary to allow for an audit of the STR’s business records unless the permittee lists exclusively on a hosting platform that has an agreement with the city that includes a provision for pass-through registration for permittees;

5.3.5    Submitted proof of insurance as required under LDC section 3-1-5-3; and

5.3.6    Has met the requirements set forth in LDC section 3-1-5-3, other applicable provisions of the City Code, these Rules, and is otherwise in good standing.

6.0    Actions by the director: denial of permits.

6.1    Generally.  The director may deny a permit or a renewal permit if:

6.1.1    The applicant fails to submit documentation as required;

6.1.2    The director determines there is or has been a material misrepresentation of fact on any document submitted by the applicant;

6.1.3    The applicant has acted or failed to act in conformance with any provision of LDC section 3-1-5-3 or these Rules including but not limited to the failure to pay any fees, taxes, or has failed to comply with any aspect of a sentence imposed by a municipal court judge relating to a case involving a Property being offered for rent, rented, or used as an STR; or

6.1.4    The designated EC does not meet the requirements of LDC section 3-1-5-3.

6.2    Notice provided.  The grounds for denying a permit will be stated in writing and provided to the applicant or the permittee at the time the action is taken or as soon thereafter as is reasonably practical.  The notice will provide information about the right to file a request for reconsideration or an appeal, as appropriate.

7.0    Permit – contents, signature.

7.1    Generally.  An STR permit will be in a form supplied by the director and must contain the:

7.1.1    STR permit number;

7.1.2    Property address;

7.1.3    Permittee’s and the EC’s names and telephone numbers;

7.1.4    Date of issuance and date of expiration of the permit;

7.1.5    Total number of bedrooms in the permitted property;

7.1.6    Total number of overnight occupants allowed to stay at the STR;

7.1.7    Total number of people allowed on the permitted property (meaning the number of overnight occupants plus no more than six additional people);

7.1.8    Location of the fire extinguisher;

7.1.9    A notice to call 911 in an emergency; and

7.1.10    Any other information deemed relevant by the director.

8.0    Abandonment of a permit.

8.1    A permit will be deemed to be abandoned under the following circumstances:

8.1.1    Failure to apply timely for renewal permit.  Understanding the time necessary to process a renewal application, and, notwithstanding any other provision of the City Code or these Rules, a permittee must submit a complete renewal application to the city not less than 45 days prior to the expiration date of the permit.  The failure by a permittee to submit a complete renewal application no less than 45 days before the expiration of the permittee’s existing permit will be deemed to be abandonment of the permit.  The abandonment will be deemed to have occurred on the day the permit expires.  The director will treat an application for renewal of a permit filed within 45 days or less of its expiration date as an application for a new STR permit.

8.1.2    Failure to rent.  A permit will be deemed abandoned if the property is not rented at least once per quarter during the term of the permit as shown on tax filings.

8.1.3    Voluntary abandonment.  A permit may be abandoned by a permittee at any time by notifying the director in writing of the permittee’s intent to abandon the permit.

8.1.4    Failure to pay lodging, sales, or other taxes due to the city.  A permit will be deemed abandoned if the permittee fails to submit lodging, sales, or other taxes due to the city in a timely manner.  The abandonment will be deemed to have occurred seven days after the taxes are due.

8.1.5    Failure to pay a renewal permit or other fee.  A permit will be deemed abandoned if the permittee fails to submit STR permitting or other fees due to the city in a timely manner.  The abandonment will be deemed to have occurred seven days after the fee is due.

9.0    Permittee responsibilities; EC responsibilities.

9.1 Generally.  The permittee is the primary point of contact for the permitted STR.  The permittee is responsible for ensuring that the STR operates in compliance with LDC section 3-1-5-3, with these Rules, and with all other applicable ordinances and laws.  In addition, the permittee is responsible for responding to STR occupant and community member complaints, inquiries and complaints from city officials, and to other on-site needs related to the STR and its operation.   

9.2 Response to complaints.  A permittee or EC must respond to a complaint within 24 hours of the time the permittee or EC is contacted about the complaint by the director or a designee thereof, by any third party STR monitoring service designated by the director to process STR complaints, or by a peace officer.   

    Exception.  A permittee or EC must respond to a noise complaint within one hour of the time the permittee or EC is contacted about the noise complaint by the director or a designee thereof, by any third party STR monitoring service designated by the director to process STR complaints, or by a peace officer. 

9.3    Reporting in person to the property.  Notwithstanding the above, a permittee must report to the property in person within one hour if asked to do so by a peace officer.  If the permittee is unable to respond to the property within one hour, the permittee may designate the EC to report to the property within one hour.  All calls from a peace officer for an in-person response to the property will first be made to the permittee at the permittee’s telephone number on file and then, if the permittee is unavailable to report to the property within one hour, to the EC at the EC’s telephone number on file.  A request to report to the property may be made via voice mail.  

9.4    Fire Protection District.  The permittee must also make available to the Arvada Fire Protection District the name and contact information for the permittee and the EC as shown on the STR permit.  The permittee must update this information with the Fire District as soon as it is practicable each time this information changes.

9.5    Emergency contact’s responsibilities.  The EC is designated by the permittee as the secondary point of contact for the permitted STR.  The EC must be able to report in person to the STR within one hour of a request to do so by a peace officer if the permittee is unable to do so.  The EC must be able to access the STR and be authorized to make decisions about the operation of the STR.  Failure of an EC to physically report to the STR within one hour of a request from a peace officer may result in enforcement action against the permittee and the EC.  Except for provisions in LDC section 3-1-5-3 or these Rules that by their very terms are only applicable to the EC, the permittee remains liable for compliance with LDC section 3-1-5-3, these Rules, and other applicable municipal and state laws.

10.0    Good Neighbor provisions.

10.1 In-unit messaging through the Good Neighbor Guide.  STRs are required to operate in accordance with all applicable city and state laws and rules.  These laws protect the health, safety, and welfare of the community and support the preservation of neighborhood character and values.  In-unit messaging about applicable laws and rules is essential to assist STR occupants in supporting the city's STR Program Rules, ensuring STRs in neighborhoods support community character, and assisting in the promotion of Arvada’s community character.  In addition to posting the STR permit, the permittee must provide within the property a copy of the “Good Neighbor Guide” as published by the director.

10.1.1 Contents.  The Guide will notify occupants that it is illegal to rent or use property that is not permitted to be an STR as an STR.  It will ask occupants to be respectful of the STR’s neighbors to help maintain the residential character of the neighborhood.  It will note that all occupants are expected to abide by all laws including the city’s noise laws that prohibit amplified sounds under Article II of Chapter 38 of the City Code.  The Guide will notify occupants that a person who fails to vacate the property upon expiration of the occupancy term may be deemed to be a trespasser and to consult their rental agreement for more detailed information about the length of their rental.  The Guide may contain any other information the director deems relevant to support neighborly practices.

10.2 Neighborhood notification.  A permittee must notify the owner or tenant of each lot that shares a property line with the lot on which the permitted STR is located that an STR permit has been issued for the permitted property.  The notice must be in writing and must contain the STR permit number and street address, the permittee’s name and contact information, and the EC’s name and contact information.  The notice must be transmitted by first class U.S. Mail within 14 days of the issuance of the permit.  Proof of notice will be considered sufficient if the permittee files with the Director within 28 days of the issuance of the permit a statement that the notice was properly transmitted.  In the case of a condominium or other multi-family dwelling, the notice must be made to the current owner or tenant of any dwelling that shares a wall, floor, or ceiling with the permitted property.  No notice is required for a lot or property that is entirely owned by a public entity, such as a city, county, or special district.

11.0    Complaints about STRs.

11.1    Generally.  An STR complaint may be filed by a member of the public, a city employee, or by a contractor hired by the city to monitor STR activity or provide other services related to the administration or enforcement of LDC section 3-1-5-3.  Those making a complaint should provide their name, their telephone number or email address, the date, time, and location of the alleged violation, and a detailed description of the action or the failure to act that constitutes the alleged violation.

11.2    Types.  A complaint about an emergency at an STR or a property thought to be offered for rent, rented, or being used as an STR may be made by calling 911.  A complaint of a non-emergency nature about activities at an STR or at a property thought to be offered for rent, rented or being used as an STR, such as a complaint about weeds, noise, or trash or snow removal, should be made through the city’s 24/7 STR telephone complaint hotline, the city’s online complaint system, or the police department’s non-emergency number.

11.3    Procedure, generally.  Complaints made through the 911 system or the police department’s non-emergency number will be handled in accordance with established police policies and procedures.  Complaints made about matters governed by the City’s building, fire, and related technical codes may be routed to the City’s code enforcement office.  Other complaints may be handled by the director or another city official as appropriate.  The director or police department may respond to an anonymous STR complaint as circumstances dictate and resources allow.  

11.4    Correction of deficiency allowed.  Except as noted in LDC section 3-1-5-3Q.1, which allows for immediate enforcement of certain violations, a peace officer or the director may contact the permittee or the EC about a complaint and ask that the situation be corrected.  In these cases, the peace officer or director may allow the permittee or the EC to correct the situation before taking additional enforcement action.

11.5    Documentation.  The director will, following consultation as necessary with a third party STR monitoring service designated by the director to process STR complaints, a peace officer, the city attorney’s office, or with any other city employee with information relevant to the case, categorize the outcome of any complaint as substantiated or unsubstantiated, and will maintain a record of the outcome.   A deficiency that is corrected after a notice from a city employee or a third party STR monitoring service designated by the director to process STR complaints but before the issuance by a peace officer of a summons and complaint in the matter will be deemed to be substantiated.  An unfounded complaint will be deemed to be unsubstantiated. 

11.6    Records.  Except for records made or maintained by the Arvada Police Department, the city clerk’s office is deemed to be the custodian of records related to STR complaints and the outcome of each complaint, including those determined to be unsubstantiated, in accordance with the city’s records retention schedule.

11.7     Fees.   The director may impose inspection, re-inspection, or other fees in connection with enforcement activities and in accordance with the City’s applicable Fees Schedules.

11.8    Coordination with marketplace facilitators.  The director will work to ensure that marketplace facilitators offer only permitted STRs for rent by requiring a marketplace facilitator to remove an advertisement or listing for a suspended, abandoned, or otherwise unavailable or unpermitted STR from their website or other digital platform.  The director may coordinate with any other city employee and any marketplace facilitator to create processes that streamline the removal of these properties from these platforms. The director’s notification should identify the listing’s uniform resource locator (URL) or other specified digital location to be removed and state the reason for the removal.  Notwithstanding the above, marketplace facilitators are still responsible for complying with all applicable sections of 3-1-5-3 of the LDC.

12.0    Administrative actions: notice and process.

12.1    Generally; when initiated.  Administrative actions are a crucial tool to help protect the quality of life in neighborhoods, maintain community character, and protect the public’s health, safety, and welfare.  The director may initiate administrative proceedings against an STR permittee where:

12.1.1 There are three substantiated complaints relating to the same STR in any one-year period;

12.1.2 There exists good cause to believe the permittee has engaged in conduct that violates a provision of this Code;

12.1.3 A permittee has been charged in the municipal court with a violation of any provision of LDC section 3-1-5-3; or

12.1.4 The permitted property has been used or allowed to be in violation of these Rules or of LDC section 3-1-5-3.

12.2 Initiation of action: contents of notice and order.  The director will initiate administrative proceedings against a permittee by mailing by first class U. S. Mail an Order to Show Cause and Notice to Appear to the permittee’s physical address shown on the permittee’s STR application.  The Order and Notice must identify the STR property by location and permit number.  It must set forth a description of the circumstances that necessitated its issuance and contain other relevant documentation, if any.   It must also note the date, time, and location of the administrative hearing on the matter.  Finally, the Order and Notice must contain a statement about the possible consequences of the failure to appear at the administrative hearing as required by the Order and Notice.

12.3 Administrative hearing.  The administrative hearing will be set within 14 days of the date of the Order and Notice.  To the extent practicable, the director will conduct the hearing under Division 3, Administrative Hearings, of Chapter 2, Administration, of the City Code.  If the permittee fails to appear at the hearing, the director will accept into the record any relevant evidence about the allegations noted in the Order and Notice and render a decision on the evidence presented.  The city has the burden of moving forward with the presentation of evidence at the hearing and bears the burden of proof to show by a preponderance of the evidence that a violation occurred.  The permittee will have the opportunity to be heard in accordance with the provisions of Division 3 of Chapter 2 noted above.

12.4 Decision.  The director will issue a written decision on the matter within 14 days of the hearing and issue it to the permittee as noted in subsection 12.2 above.  In making their decision, the director may consider any relevant information presented at the hearing, including but not limited to the permittee’s past history and any actions the permittee has taken to remedy the violation.  The justification for imposing administrative consequences, if any, on the permittee will be stated in the decision.  The decision must set forth the permittee’s appeal rights.

12.5 Administrative consequences.  The director is authorized to impose the following consequences for a substantiated violation of any provision of the City Code or these Rules:

12.5.1 First violation: Suspension of the STR permit for up to 90 consecutive days.

12.5.2 Second violation within any one-year period: Suspension of the STR permit for up to 180 consecutive days.

12.5.3 Third violation within any one-year period: Revocation of the STR permit. 

12.5.4 Notwithstanding the above, the director may revoke a permit for a violation listed in LDC section 3-1-5-3Q1 that has been committed by the permittee or for which the permittee is responsible.  The director may impose other conditions in connection with the suspension of an STR permit.  A suspension or revocation may begin at any time after the director’s decision is issued.

12.6 Calculating dates of violation.  The operative date for calculating the date of the second or third violation under this section 12 is the date of the violation or violations.

13.0 Requests for reconsideration; appeals.

13.1    Reconsideration.  An applicant or permittee may request reconsideration by the director of a decision to deny a permit or not renew a permit by filing with the director a form prescribed by the city and available from the director.  The reconsideration form must be filed within seven days of the date of the director’s decision, state with specificity the error claimed, and contain documentation supporting the claim, if any.  Any grounds or basis not stated on the request form will not be considered as part of the request.  A reconsideration request may be granted upon a showing that the decision was based upon a clear or plain error of fact or law.  After consultation with the city attorney’s office, the director will, within seven days of the date the request was filed, review the request and city records related to the request and issue a written decision on the request.  The decision must contain the reason or reasons for the decision and must set forth the permittee’s appeal rights.  The decision will be issued to the permittee as noted in subsection 12.2 above.  Should the director fail to issue a written decision within seven days, the claim will be deemed denied, and the applicant or permittee may appeal as set forth herein.

13.2    Appeal.  An applicant or permittee may appeal a decision to deny a request for reconsideration or a decision to suspend or revoke a permit by filing with the city manager an appeal form prescribed by the city and available from the director.  The appeal form must be filed within 14 days of the date of the director’s decision, must state with specificity the grounds or basis for the appeal, and contain documentation supporting the claim, if any.  Any grounds or basis not stated on the appeal form will not be considered as part of the appeal.

    Exception.  A STR permittee or the owner of a property being offered for rent, rented, or being used as an STR may appeal a decision by the director or designee thereof to post a property with a red tag notice by making application to the building code advisory board in accordance with the provisions of Arvada City Code section 18-36, Board of Appeals.

13.3 Action by city manager.  All information submitted as part of the appeal will be submitted to the city manager or designee thereof.  Notwithstanding any other provision of the Code, the appeal of the director’s decision shall consist of a review of all relevant documentation submitted by the applicant or permittee, as well as any other information on file with the city in the matter.  All documentation may be reviewed electronically.

13.3.1 Process.  The city bears the burden of proof in an appeal to show that the director’s decision was supported by substantial and competent evidence.  The manager will review the documentation submitted, and, within 14 days of the filing of the appeal, issue a written decision on the appeal.  The decision must contain the reason or reasons for the decision and set forth the permittee’s appeal rights.  The decision will be issued to the permittee as noted in subsection 12.2 above.  Should the city manager fail to issue a written decision on the appeal within 14 days, the appeal will be deemed denied, and the permittee may proceed as if an adverse decision had been issued.

13.4 Appeal of the manager’s decision.  The city manager’s decision, or denial of an appeal because of the manager’s failure to issue a written decision, shall be considered a final agency decision.  The appeal may proceed under Rule 106 of the Colorado Rules of Civil Procedure.

13.5 Concurrent actions.  Nothing in these Rules precludes concurrent administrative, civil, investigative, or enforcement actions or as set forth in LDC section 3-1-5-3.

14.0    Fees.  As is the case with all city fees, STR fees will reflect the direct and indirect costs of providing services reasonably necessary to implement and administer the STR ordinance and the STR Program Rules, including but not limited to the costs of reviewing and processing applications, inspection costs, and similar costs.  STR fees must be remitted at the time the permit application is submitted or at the time a service is requested or rendered, whichever is earliest.  Fees are not refundable and will not be pro-rated, even if a permit is suspended, revoked, or abandoned
